    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d502">

                <pb facs="0502" n="494" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg494"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890G.00/545</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Telephone Conversation, by Mr. Gordon P.
                        Merriam of the Division of Near Eastern Affairs</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-04-07">April 7, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row>
                        <cell rendition="#nowrap #top">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Nevile Butler, Counselor of the
                            British Embassy</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Murray<anchor n="19" xml:id="d502-f1"
                            /></cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>Mr. Murray referred to the British Embassy’s <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> of April 5, 1941, in which the hope was expressed that
                    this Government would withhold recognition from the new Gailani Government in
                    Iraq and so instruct its representative in Baghdad. Mr. Murray stated that we
                    had just received a telegram from our Minister in Baghdad,<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d502-f2"/> apparently based upon a somewhat different view of the
                    situation from that which had prompted the Embassy’s <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi>, to the effect that in the opinion of the British
                    Ambassador there, a formal decision by the British Government either to
                    recognize or not to recognize the Gailani regime was open to serious objections.
                    It therefore seemed undesirable for us to take a step which in the opinion of
                    Sir Kinahan Cornwallis it would be unwise for his own Government to take at this
                    time.</p>
                <p>Mr. Butler agreed.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
